// Domain 4: Security and Compliance (16%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d4-01", d: 4,
  q: "A company requires IAM users to sign in with MFA before they can do anything other than manage their own MFA device. Some users sign in without MFA and can still stop Amazon EC2 instances.\n\nWhich IAM policy approach will enforce this requirement?",
  o: ["Attach a policy that denies all but MFA self-management actions when aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is false.",
      "Attach a policy that allows all actions only when the aws:SecureTransport condition key is set to true.",
      "Enable an account password policy that requires users to change their passwords every 30 days.",
      "Attach a policy that denies all actions whenever the aws:RequestedRegion condition key does not equal us-east-1."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Deny statement with a condition such as BoolIfExists aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent false blocks every action except the ones a user needs to set up MFA. After the user signs in with MFA, the condition no longer matches, and the user's other permissions apply.",
      "aws:SecureTransport checks whether a request used TLS. It has nothing to do with MFA.",
      "A password policy controls password strength and rotation. It does not require MFA.",
      "aws:RequestedRegion restricts Regions. It does not check whether the user signed in with MFA."] },

{ id: "d4-02", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an application on an Amazon EC2 instance that reads objects from an Amazon S3 bucket. The developers stored an IAM user's access keys in the application's configuration file. A security review flags this as a risk.\n\nWhich solution will remove this risk in the MOST secure manner?",
  o: ["Attach an IAM role with read access to the bucket through an instance profile. Delete the keys.",
      "Encrypt the configuration file with AWS KMS.",
      "Move the access keys into environment variables on the instance. Remove them from the file.",
      "Rotate the access keys every 90 days. Store the new keys in the same configuration file."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An instance profile gives the application temporary credentials that rotate automatically through the instance metadata service. The SDK picks them up with no keys in files, and the long-lived keys can be deleted.",
      "The application still has to decrypt and use long-lived keys, so the keys can still leak and never expire.",
      "Environment variables still hold long-lived keys that can leak through logs or process listings.",
      "Rotation limits how long a key is exposed, but the keys are still long-lived and stored on the instance."] },

{ id: "d4-03", d: 4,
  q: "A company's third-party monitoring vendor needs read-only access to the company's AWS account. The vendor serves many customers and calls the company's account from the vendor's own AWS account.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer grant access in the MOST secure manner?",
  o: ["Create an IAM role that trusts the vendor's account. Require a unique external ID in the trust policy.",
      "Create an IAM user for the vendor with read-only permissions. Send the access keys by email.",
      "Share the company account's root credentials with the vendor.",
      "Add the vendor's account to the company's Amazon S3 bucket policies. Allow all read actions on all buckets."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A cross-account role lets the vendor use temporary credentials from its own account. Requiring a unique external ID in the trust policy (the sts:ExternalId condition) protects against the confused deputy problem, where another of the vendor's customers could trick the vendor into accessing your account.",
      "Long-lived access keys sent by email can leak and must be rotated by hand, and they are not needed for cross-account access.",
      "Root credentials must never be shared. They have unrestricted access to the account.",
      "Bucket policies cover only Amazon S3, and monitoring needs read access across many services."] },

{ id: "d4-04", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 25 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and 2,000 employees in Microsoft Entra ID. Employees must sign in with their corporate credentials and get access to accounts based on their Entra ID groups. The company must stop using IAM users.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?",
  o: ["Connect IAM Identity Center to Entra ID with SAML and SCIM, and assign permission sets to groups.",
      "Create an IAM user in every account for each employee, and sync their passwords from Entra ID every night.",
      "Create a SAML identity provider and roles in each of the 25 accounts, and map groups in each one.",
      "Create one IAM user per team in the management account. Share the passwords with each team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["IAM Identity Center provides single sign-on to all accounts in the organization. SAML federates sign-in with Entra ID, SCIM provisions users and groups automatically, and permission sets that are assigned to groups create the roles in each account.",
      "This keeps thousands of IAM users with long-lived credentials, which the company wants to stop.",
      "Per-account SAML federation works, but it must be built and maintained in 25 accounts, which is more overhead.",
      "Shared credentials break accountability and least privilege."] },

{ id: "d4-05", d: 4,
  q: "A company stores sensitive data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The bucket must be accessible only through a specific gateway VPC endpoint in the company's VPC, even by users who have IAM permissions to the bucket.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies all S3 actions when aws:SourceVpce does not equal the endpoint ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy that allows access only when aws:SourceIp matches the VPC's CIDR block.",
      "Attach a security group to the bucket that allows inbound HTTPS only from the gateway VPC endpoint.",
      "Add a route in the VPC route table that sends all traffic for the bucket to a NAT gateway."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Deny statement with a StringNotEquals condition on aws:SourceVpce blocks every request that does not arrive through that endpoint. An explicit deny overrides any allow, so even users with IAM permissions cannot reach the bucket from anywhere else.",
      "aws:SourceIp does not contain private VPC addresses for requests that arrive through a VPC endpoint, so this condition would not work as intended.",
      "S3 buckets do not have security groups.",
      "A NAT gateway sends traffic to the public S3 endpoint, which does not limit access to the VPC endpoint."] },

{ id: "d4-06", d: 4,
  q: "A company must keep all workloads in eu-central-1 and eu-west-1 for data residency. Users in member accounts must not be able to create resources in other AWS Regions, but global services such as IAM and Amazon Route 53 must continue to work.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Attach an SCP that denies requests to other Regions by using aws:RequestedRegion, with global services exempted.",
      "Attach an IAM policy to each IAM user and role that allows actions only in eu-central-1 and eu-west-1.",
      "Configure AWS Config in all other Regions with a rule that deletes any new resources that it finds.",
      "Opt out of every other Region for each member account from the account settings in the management account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An SCP that denies aws:RequestedRegion values other than the two approved Regions sets a guardrail for every principal in member accounts. Exempting global services such as IAM, AWS Organizations, Route 53, CloudFront, and AWS Support keeps them working, because their requests go to us-east-1.",
      "IAM policies on each identity are easy to miss or change, and new roles would not be covered automatically.",
      "Deleting resources after they are created is reactive, and data might already be stored in the wrong Region.",
      "Only opt-in Regions can be disabled. Regions that are enabled by default cannot be disabled, so this cannot block them."] },

{ id: "d4-07", d: 4,
  q: "A company allows developers to create IAM roles for their AWS Lambda functions. The company's security team must ensure that any role a developer creates can never have more than a defined set of permissions, even if the developer attaches AdministratorAccess to the role.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Require a permissions boundary on every role that developers create, enforced with an IAM condition key.",
      "Attach an SCP to the account that denies the developers access to the iam:CreateRole action.",
      "Enable IAM Access Analyzer to delete any role with too many permissions automatically every day.",
      "Ask developers to use only AWS managed policies when they attach policies to their new roles."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A permissions boundary sets the maximum permissions that identity-based policies can grant to a role. If the developers' own policy allows iam:CreateRole only when the iam:PermissionsBoundary condition matches the approved boundary, every role they create is capped, even with AdministratorAccess attached.",
      "Denying role creation stops developers from doing their work, which is not the goal.",
      "Access Analyzer reports findings. It does not delete roles or cap their permissions.",
      "AWS managed policies include AdministratorAccess, and a request to developers is not an enforced control."] },

{ id: "d4-08", d: 4,
  q: "A company has hundreds of projects that share one AWS account. Engineers must be able to start and stop only the Amazon EC2 instances that belong to their own project. New projects are added every week, and the company's security team does not want to update IAM policies for each new project.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Tag instances and roles with Project. Allow actions only when the resource and principal Project tags match.",
      "Create an IAM policy for each project that lists the instance IDs of that project's instances. Update it weekly.",
      "Create a separate VPC for each project. Allow the engineers to manage instances only inside their own VPC.",
      "Create an IAM group for each project. Add a new group with its own policy every week for each new project."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Attribute-based access control (ABAC) uses tags in policy conditions. One policy that compares aws:ResourceTag/Project with aws:PrincipalTag/Project works for every project, including new ones, as long as resources and roles are tagged correctly. Engineers must not be allowed to change the Project tag.",
      "Per-project policies with instance IDs must change whenever instances or projects change, which is the work the team wants to avoid.",
      "IAM does not easily scope EC2 start and stop actions by VPC, and a VPC for each project adds network overhead.",
      "A new group and policy every week is the manual work the team wants to avoid."] },

{ id: "d4-09", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security review finds that the root user of an AWS account has active access keys and no MFA device. Administrators sign in through AWS IAM Identity Center for their daily work.\n\nWhich combination of steps follows AWS best practices? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Delete the access keys for the root user.",
      "Configure MFA for the root user.",
      "Rotate the root user's access keys every 90 days.",
      "Store the root user's access keys in AWS Secrets Manager.",
      "Attach a permissions boundary to the root user."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Root user access keys grant unrestricted access to the account and should not exist. Delete them, and use roles for programmatic access.",
      "MFA protects the most powerful identity in the account from a stolen password. In AWS Organizations, centralized root access management can also remove root credentials from member accounts.",
      "Rotation shortens how long a key is valid, but the root user should have no access keys at all.",
      "Storing the keys more safely still leaves long-lived root credentials that should not exist.",
      "Permissions boundaries apply to IAM users and roles. They cannot limit the root user."] },

{ id: "d4-10", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an application role that sometimes receives AccessDenied errors from AWS KMS when it reads encrypted objects from Amazon S3. A CloudOps engineer needs to find which KMS key, which API action, and which principal were involved in each failure.\n\nWhat should the CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Search AWS CloudTrail for kms.amazonaws.com events with errorCode AccessDenied, and review their details.",
      "Enable S3 server access logging, and wait for the next failure to see the error in the logs.",
      "Run the IAM policy simulator against every KMS key in the account with every possible KMS action.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the application's subnets, and look for rejected traffic going to AWS KMS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail records KMS API calls, including failed ones, with the errorCode, the action (such as Decrypt), the key ARN, and the userIdentity of the caller. CloudTrail Lake or CloudWatch Logs Insights can filter these events quickly.",
      "Server access logs show S3 requests, but they do not show the KMS call details or the key that denied access.",
      "Simulating every key and action is slow and would not show what actually failed.",
      "Flow logs show network traffic. AccessDenied is a permissions error, not a network problem."] },

{ id: "d4-11", d: 4,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer needs to attach a new IAM policy to a production role. Before attaching the policy, the CloudOps engineer must confirm that the role will be allowed to call s3:PutObject on one bucket and denied on all other buckets. The check must account for the SCPs and permissions boundary that apply to the role.\n\nWhich tool should the CloudOps engineer use?",
  o: ["The IAM policy simulator",
      "AWS Trusted Advisor checks",
      "Amazon Inspector findings",
      "AWS Config rule evaluations"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The IAM policy simulator tests identity-based policies, permissions boundaries, resource-based policies, and SCPs against chosen actions and resources. It shows whether each request would be allowed or denied without making real requests.",
      "Trusted Advisor checks for best practices, such as unrestricted security groups. It does not evaluate specific policy decisions.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities and network exposure. It does not test IAM policies.",
      "Config evaluates resource configurations against rules. It does not simulate API authorization."] },

{ id: "d4-12", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security team must be notified whenever an Amazon S3 bucket, AWS KMS key, IAM role, or AWS Lambda function in any of the organization's accounts is shared with a principal outside the organization.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an organization external access analyzer in IAM Access Analyzer. Route findings to Amazon SNS with Amazon EventBridge.",
      "Write a Lambda function that reads every resource policy each hour and compares the principals with the organization.",
      "Configure Amazon GuardDuty in every account, and alert on findings that show data being read from S3 buckets.",
      "Use AWS Trusted Advisor to check bucket permissions weekly, and email the results to the security team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An external access analyzer that uses the organization as its zone of trust analyzes resource-based policies and reports findings for resources shared outside the organization. Findings are sent to EventBridge, so a rule can notify the team through Amazon SNS.",
      "This is custom code that must understand complex policy logic for many resource types.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats from activity. It does not analyze resource policies for external sharing.",
      "The Trusted Advisor check covers only S3 bucket permissions, and a weekly email is not timely."] },

{ id: "d4-13", d: 4,
  q: "A company must find IAM roles that have not been used for 90 days, and permissions inside roles that are never used, so that the company can remove them and enforce least privilege across all of its accounts.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an unused access analyzer in IAM Access Analyzer with a 90-day tracking period.",
      "Create an external access analyzer in IAM Access Analyzer with the account as the zone of trust.",
      "Search the AWS CloudTrail event history in each account for roles that have no events in 90 days.",
      "Configure AWS Config. Use a managed rule that deletes IAM roles that are older than 90 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An unused access analyzer reports unused roles, unused access keys and passwords, and unused service and action permissions, based on a tracking period that you choose. It can cover the whole organization and gives findings that you can act on.",
      "External access analyzers find resources shared outside the zone of trust. They do not report unused permissions.",
      "Event history keeps only 90 days of management events for each Region, and analyzing it by hand across all accounts is slow and incomplete.",
      "A role's age does not show whether it is used, and deleting roles automatically could break workloads."] },

{ id: "d4-14", d: 4,
  q: "A company has a new application role with broad permissions. The application has run in a test account for 30 days with AWS CloudTrail enabled. The company needs a least-privilege policy based on what the application actually used.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate a policy from the role's CloudTrail activity. Review it.",
      "Use the IAM policy simulator to generate a policy that allows every action the role has access to.",
      "Attach the ReadOnlyAccess managed policy. Add actions back when the application fails.",
      "Use AWS Config to record the role's permissions. Copy the recorded policy to production."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Access Analyzer policy generation reviews the role's CloudTrail activity for a chosen period and creates a policy with the services and actions that the role actually used. The team reviews and refines the policy before attaching it.",
      "The simulator tests existing policies. It does not generate policies, and allowing every action is not least privilege.",
      "Fixing failures one at a time in production causes outages and slow progress.",
      "Config records the current broad policy, so copying it does not reduce the permissions."] },

{ id: "d4-15", d: 4,
  q: "A user in a member account has an IAM policy that allows ec2:RunInstances on all resources. When the user tries to launch a p4d.24xlarge Amazon EC2 instance, the request fails with an explicit deny, but launches of t3 instances work. No permissions boundary is attached to the user.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["An SCP that applies to the account denies ec2:RunInstances for certain instance types.",
      "The user's IAM policy is missing the ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes action for large types.",
      "The account has an On-Demand Instance quota that is too low for the p4d instance family.",
      "The p4d.24xlarge instance type requires the user to sign in with the AWS account root user."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An explicit deny that the user's own policy does not explain often comes from a service control policy. SCPs can deny actions with conditions such as ec2:InstanceType, and an explicit deny overrides the IAM allow. Decoding the authorization message shows which kind of policy denied the request.",
      "Describe permissions are not needed to launch a specific instance type, and a missing permission would cause an implicit deny, not an explicit deny.",
      "A quota problem returns a limit exceeded error, not an access denied error.",
      "Launching instances never requires the root user."] },

{ id: "d4-16", d: 4,
  q: "An Amazon EC2 API call fails with an error that includes an encoded authorization failure message. A CloudOps engineer needs to see which policy statement denied the request.\n\nWhat should the CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Run aws sts decode-authorization-message with the encoded message.",
      "Run aws iam simulate-custom-policy with the encoded message text.",
      "Base64 decode the message locally to read the policy statement.",
      "Paste the encoded message into the AWS CloudTrail console to decode it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["STS DecodeAuthorizationMessage turns the encoded message into readable details about the request, including the matched statements and whether the deny was explicit. The caller needs the sts:DecodeAuthorizationMessage permission.",
      "The policy simulator tests policies that you give it. It does not decode authorization messages.",
      "The message is encrypted, not just encoded, so it cannot be read with a simple Base64 decode.",
      "CloudTrail records the failed call, but it does not decode this message."] },

{ id: "d4-17", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an Amazon S3 bucket in account A. The bucket's objects are encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key in account A. A role in account B has an IAM policy that allows s3:GetObject on the bucket, and the bucket policy allows the role. Downloads from account B continue to fail with AccessDenied.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Allow the account B role to use kms:Decrypt in the key policy. Allow kms:Decrypt in the role's IAM policy.",
      "Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3 for new objects, and leave the existing objects encrypted as they are.",
      "Enable S3 Object Ownership with the bucket owner preferred setting.",
      "Add the account B role to the bucket's ACL with full control, in addition to the existing bucket policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["To read objects encrypted with SSE-KMS, the caller needs permission to use the key. Across accounts, the key policy in account A must allow the account B role (or account B), and the role's IAM policy in account B must also allow kms:Decrypt on the key ARN.",
      "Existing objects stay encrypted with the KMS key, so reads of them would still fail.",
      "Object Ownership controls who owns uploaded objects. It does not grant KMS permissions.",
      "ACLs grant S3 permissions only, and S3 access is already allowed. The missing piece is the KMS key permission."] },

{ id: "d4-18", d: 4,
  q: "A company attaches an SCP to the organization root that denies stopping AWS CloudTrail trails. During a test, a user in the management account can still stop a trail.\n\nWhy can the user stop the trail?",
  o: ["SCPs do not affect users and roles in the management account.",
      "SCPs apply only to the root users of the member accounts.",
      "SCPs take 24 hours to apply after they are attached to the root.",
      "SCPs apply only to resources created after the SCP is attached."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Service control policies never restrict the management account, even when they are attached to the organization root. Sensitive actions there must be controlled with IAM policies, and workloads should run in member accounts.",
      "SCPs apply to all IAM users and roles in member accounts, including the root user of those accounts.",
      "SCPs take effect quickly after they are attached. There is no 24-hour delay.",
      "SCPs restrict actions on all resources, no matter when the resources were created."] },

{ id: "d4-19", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security team must manage Amazon GuardDuty, AWS Security Hub CSPM, and IAM Access Analyzer for all accounts from a dedicated security account instead of the management account.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Register the security account as the delegated administrator for each service in Organizations.",
      "Move the security account to the root OU.",
      "Create IAM users in the management account for the security team. Use them for each service.",
      "Share the management account's root credentials with the security team for these three services."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Many security services support a delegated administrator. Registering the security account as the delegated administrator lets it enable and manage the service for all member accounts, so the security team does not need to work in the management account.",
      "OU placement does not grant administrative powers over other accounts.",
      "This keeps the team working in the management account, which AWS recommends limiting to essential tasks.",
      "Root credentials must never be shared."] },

{ id: "d4-20", d: 4,
  q: "A company is setting up a new multi-account environment. New accounts must be created with a standard baseline, central logging, and preventive and detective guardrails that are applied automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Set up AWS Control Tower. Create accounts through Account Factory in governed OUs.",
      "Create accounts with the Organizations API. Run a script that configures each new account.",
      "Create accounts manually, and ask each account owner to follow a written security checklist.",
      "Create one account for every workload. Use IAM policies as the only guardrails."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Control Tower sets up a landing zone with log archive and audit accounts, and Account Factory creates new accounts with a standard baseline. Controls are applied to OUs as preventive SCPs, detective AWS Config rules, and proactive CloudFormation hooks.",
      "Scripts can work, but they are custom tooling to build and maintain.",
      "Manual setup and checklists are inconsistent, and they do not enforce guardrails.",
      "IAM policies alone can be changed by account administrators, and they give no organization-wide guardrails or central logging."] },

{ id: "d4-21", d: 4,
  q: "A company's finance team requires every new Amazon EC2 instance to have a CostCenter tag with one of five approved values. Launches that do not include a valid tag must be blocked in all accounts in the organization.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a tag policy that defines CostCenter and its allowed values, with enforcement for EC2 instances.",
      "Create an SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances when aws:RequestTag/CostCenter is missing.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that deletes instances without a CostCenter tag once a day.",
      "Ask each team to add the CostCenter tag in the console when they launch instances.",
      "Enable cost allocation tags in the Billing console for the CostCenter tag key only."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Tag policies standardize tag keys and values across the organization. With enforcement enabled for a resource type, a request that sets the tag to a value that is not allowed is blocked.",
      "Tag policies do not block resources that are created without the tag. An SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances when the aws:RequestTag/CostCenter key is null makes the tag required at launch.",
      "Deleting instances after they launch is disruptive, and it does not block the launch.",
      "A request to teams is not enforced, and it does not cover the CLI or automation.",
      "Cost allocation tags make tags appear in billing reports. They do not require or validate tags."] },

{ id: "d4-22", d: 4,
  q: "A company stores shared reports in an Amazon S3 bucket. Applications in every account in the company's organization in AWS Organizations must be able to read the reports, and no one outside the organization can have access. New accounts are added often.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that allows s3:GetObject when aws:PrincipalOrgID matches the organization ID.",
      "Add a bucket policy that lists the ID of every member account. Update it for each new account.",
      "Create an IAM role in the bucket's account. Have the applications in each account assume it.",
      "Disable S3 Block Public Access. Allow s3:GetObject for all principals in the bucket policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The aws:PrincipalOrgID condition key checks that the caller belongs to the organization, so new accounts are covered automatically and no account list is needed. Roles in other accounts still need IAM permissions to read the bucket. Resource control policies (RCPs) can enforce a similar boundary across many resources.",
      "An account list works, but someone must update it every time an account is added, which is the overhead the company wants to avoid.",
      "A shared role adds setup in every account and changes how each application reaches the reports.",
      "This makes the bucket public, so anyone on the internet could read the reports."] },

{ id: "d4-23", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses a bastion host with SSH keys to administer Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets. The company's security team must remove the bastion host and all inbound SSH rules. The security team also needs a record of each session.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager with an instance profile on each instance. Send session logs to Amazon S3.",
      "Move the bastion host to a public subnet. Restrict inbound SSH to the office IP range, and log each SSH command.",
      "Use Amazon EC2 Instance Connect to push temporary SSH keys. Keep inbound port 22 open to the VPC CIDR block.",
      "Create an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection to the VPC. Allow inbound SSH from the on-premises network to the instances."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Session Manager needs no open inbound ports, no bastion hosts, and no SSH keys. Access is controlled through IAM, and session logs can go to Amazon S3 or Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The instances need an instance profile with the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy.",
      "This keeps the bastion host and the inbound SSH rules, which the security team must remove.",
      "Instance Connect avoids long-term keys, but it still uses SSH and an inbound rule on port 22.",
      "A VPN changes the network path, but the inbound SSH rules and SSH keys remain."] },

{ id: "d4-24", d: 4,
  q: "A company's engineers sign in to AWS through AWS IAM Identity Center. The engineers now need to run AWS CLI commands against several accounts, and they must not create IAM access keys.\n\nWhat should the engineers do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Configure an AWS CLI profile for IAM Identity Center. Run aws sso login to get temporary credentials.",
      "Create an IAM user with access keys in each account. Add the keys to the AWS CLI configuration file.",
      "Copy the session cookie from the AWS access portal into the AWS CLI configuration on each workstation.",
      "Ask an administrator to share the management account's access keys with all of the engineers' profiles."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS CLI supports IAM Identity Center profiles. aws sso login opens a browser sign-in, and the CLI then gets short-lived credentials for the chosen account and permission set, so no access keys are needed.",
      "This creates long-lived access keys, which the engineers must avoid.",
      "Session cookies are not CLI credentials, and copying them is insecure.",
      "Sharing access keys breaks least privilege and accountability."] },

{ id: "d4-25", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS Organizations. The company's security team must ensure that no one in any member account, including account administrators, can stop AWS CloudTrail logging or delete a trail.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Attach an SCP to the member accounts' OUs that denies cloudtrail:StopLogging and cloudtrail:DeleteTrail.",
      "Attach an IAM policy that denies those actions to every IAM user and role in each of the member accounts.",
      "Enable CloudTrail log file integrity validation.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that reports when CloudTrail logging is stopped in any member account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SCPs set the maximum permissions for every principal in member accounts, including administrators and the root user, and account administrators cannot remove them. An organization trail also helps, because member accounts cannot change it.",
      "Administrators in each account could detach or edit the policy, and new roles would not be covered automatically.",
      "Validation proves whether log files changed after delivery. It does not stop anyone from disabling logging.",
      "Config can detect and report the change, but it does not prevent it."] },

{ id: "d4-26", d: 4,
  q: "A company's AWS Trusted Advisor dashboard shows a red security check for security groups that allow unrestricted access (0.0.0.0/0) to port 22 on several production instances. Administrators use SSH only for occasional troubleshooting.\n\nWhich solution will resolve the finding and keep administrative access in the MOST secure manner?",
  o: ["Remove the SSH rules. Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager for administrative access.",
      "Change the SSH rules to allow 0.0.0.0/0 on port 2222 instead of port 22 on the instances.",
      "Keep the SSH rules. Enable Amazon GuardDuty to detect brute force SSH attempts.",
      "Keep the SSH rules. Suppress the AWS Trusted Advisor check for these security groups."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Session Manager gives shell access through SSM Agent with no inbound ports and no SSH keys, and sessions are controlled by IAM and can be logged. Removing the open SSH rules removes the exposure that Trusted Advisor flagged.",
      "Moving SSH to another port still leaves it open to the whole internet.",
      "GuardDuty detects attacks, but the open port remains exposed.",
      "Hiding the finding does not fix the exposure."] },

{ id: "d4-27", d: 4,
  q: "AWS notifies a company that an IAM user's access key was found in a public GitHub repository. The AWS Trusted Advisor Exposed Access Keys check is also red for the key.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do FIRST?",
  o: ["Deactivate the exposed access key, and replace it with a new key or a role for the workload.",
      "Make the GitHub repository private to stop others from seeing the access key from now on.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail Insights to report unusual API activity from the key in the future.",
      "Add the key to an IAM policy that denies it only for requests that come from outside the VPC."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An exposed key must be treated as compromised. Deactivating it stops further use right away. The team should then replace it, preferably with temporary credentials from a role, review CloudTrail for misuse, and delete the old key.",
      "The key has already been seen and may already be in use by attackers. Hiding the repository does not revoke it.",
      "Detecting unusual activity later does not stop an attacker who already has the key.",
      "IAM policies cannot target a single access key in this way, and the key would still be valid."] },

{ id: "d4-28", d: 4,
  q: "A company must receive a notification and must start remediation automatically whenever the status of an AWS Trusted Advisor security check changes to red in its account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for Trusted Advisor check status changes in us-east-1 with Amazon SNS and AWS Lambda targets.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm that watches the Trusted Advisor console page, and refresh the page every 5 minutes.",
      "Configure AWS Config in us-east-1. Create an AWS Config rule for each of the Trusted Advisor security checks.",
      "Export the Trusted Advisor report to Amazon S3 weekly, and run an AWS Lambda function that compares the reports."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Trusted Advisor sends check status change events to EventBridge in the us-east-1 Region. A rule that matches red security checks can notify the team through Amazon SNS and invoke a Lambda function or an Automation runbook to remediate.",
      "CloudWatch alarms watch metrics, not web pages.",
      "Config rules evaluate resource configurations. They do not receive Trusted Advisor check results.",
      "Weekly comparisons are slow and are custom code to maintain."] },

{ id: "d4-29", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS Organizations. An auditor requires 40 accounts to be continuously evaluated against a common set of controls based on the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, with results for each account.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement in the MOST operationally efficient way?",
  o: ["Deploy an AWS Config conformance pack based on the CIS template to all accounts from the delegated administrator.",
      "Write custom AWS Lambda functions for each CIS control, and run them in every account of the organization once a week.",
      "Run Amazon Inspector in every account, and export the vulnerability findings to the auditor each month.",
      "Configure AWS CloudTrail in every account, and ask the auditor to read through the trail logs for each of the controls."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A conformance pack is a collection of AWS Config rules and remediation actions that is deployed as one unit. Sample templates exist for frameworks such as CIS, and organization conformance packs deploy them to all member accounts and report compliance by account.",
      "Custom functions for every control are a lot of code to build and maintain, and weekly runs are not continuous.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities and network exposure. It does not evaluate CIS account configuration controls.",
      "Logs alone do not evaluate controls. The auditor would have to analyze configurations by hand."] },

{ id: "d4-30", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses AWS Config in 30 accounts and 3 AWS Regions. The company's compliance team needs a single view of noncompliant resources across all of those accounts and Regions.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Config aggregator for the organization in a central account.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch dashboard in each account that shows Config compliance data.",
      "Copy every Config snapshot to one Amazon S3 bucket, and read the files manually each week.",
      "Create a Config rule in the central account that evaluates resources in all accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An aggregator collects AWS Config configuration and compliance data from multiple accounts and Regions, or from a whole organization, into one account. The team can view and query noncompliant resources in one place.",
      "Per-account dashboards do not give a single cross-account view.",
      "Reading snapshot files by hand is slow and does not scale.",
      "Config rules evaluate resources in their own account and Region. An aggregator is needed to combine the results."] },

{ id: "d4-31", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an AWS Config managed rule that marks security groups that allow SSH from 0.0.0.0/0 as noncompliant. The company's security team needs these SSH rules removed automatically within minutes of detection.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add an automatic remediation action to the Config rule that runs the AWS-DisablePublicAccessForSecurityGroup runbook.",
      "Create an AWS Lambda function that runs every hour, lists every security group in every Region, and removes any open SSH rules.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty to remove the open SSH rules when it detects SSH brute force activity.",
      "Email the noncompliant resources to the security team each day to fix them manually."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Config rules can have remediation actions that run Systems Manager Automation runbooks. With automatic remediation enabled, Config runs the runbook, such as AWS-DisablePublicAccessForSecurityGroup, on each noncompliant security group by using an IAM role that you provide.",
      "A scheduled function is custom code and runs only hourly, so open rules could exist for up to an hour.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats. It does not change security groups by itself.",
      "Manual fixes are slow, and open SSH rules would stay in place for up to a day."] },

{ id: "d4-32", d: 4,
  q: "A company's policy requires every Amazon EBS volume to have a DataClass tag and to be encrypted. The company needs one AWS Config rule that checks both conditions together, but no managed rule does this.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer create to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["An AWS Config custom rule that uses AWS CloudFormation Guard or an AWS Lambda function",
      "An AWS CloudTrail Insights selector for all of the EBS volume creation events",
      "An AWS Trusted Advisor custom check that covers each of the EBS volumes",
      "An Amazon CloudWatch alarm on the VolumeQueueLength metric for each volume"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When no managed rule fits, a custom rule can evaluate configuration items with a policy written in CloudFormation Guard, or with a Lambda function for more complex logic. One rule can check both the tag and the encryption setting.",
      "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity rates. It does not evaluate resource configurations.",
      "Trusted Advisor does not support custom checks of this kind.",
      "VolumeQueueLength measures pending I/O. It says nothing about tags or encryption."] },

{ id: "d4-33", d: 4,
  q: "A company must be able to determine how each of its security groups was configured at any point in time. The company also must evaluate each configuration change against company rules.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Enable AWS Config to record security groups. Create AWS Config rules, and review the resource timeline for each security group.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail Insights on the account's trail. Review the Insights events that are recorded for each security group.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for each network interface. Run Amazon Athena queries against the logs to review accepted connections.",
      "Review the AWS Trusted Advisor security checks each week. Export the results of each check to an Amazon S3 bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Config records how resources are configured and how they change over time, and the resource timeline shows each past configuration. AWS Config rules evaluate each change against the company's desired settings. CloudTrail shows who made the API call, while AWS Config shows the resulting configuration.",
      "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API call rates. It does not keep the configuration of a security group at each point in time.",
      "Flow logs show traffic that the security groups allowed or rejected, not the rules that were configured.",
      "Trusted Advisor checks show current best-practice results. They do not provide a history of each configuration change."] },

{ id: "d4-34", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 2,000 Amazon S3 buckets across many accounts. The company's security team needs to find which buckets contain personally identifiable information (PII), such as passport numbers and credit card numbers, and must continue to check as the data changes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Configure Amazon Macie with automated sensitive data discovery for the organization.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection to inspect every object for sensitive data.",
      "Run Amazon Athena queries over every bucket each week to search for PII patterns.",
      "Use S3 Inventory reports to list object names that contain the words passport or card."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Macie uses managed and custom data identifiers to find sensitive data, such as PII and financial data, in Amazon S3. Automated sensitive data discovery samples objects across buckets continuously and keeps a sensitivity score for each bucket, across the organization.",
      "GuardDuty S3 Protection detects suspicious access to S3. It does not inspect object contents for sensitive data.",
      "Athena works only on data that you define tables for, and weekly custom queries across 2,000 buckets are a lot of work.",
      "Object names do not show what the objects contain."] },

{ id: "d4-35", d: 4,
  q: "A company must continuously scan its Amazon EC2 instances and Amazon ECR container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure. New resources must be included automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Enable Amazon Inspector in the account. Review the findings for the EC2 instances and the container images.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty in the account. Review the threat findings for the EC2 instances and the container images.",
      "Enable Amazon Macie in the account. Review the sensitive data findings for the EC2 instances and the container images.",
      "Enable AWS Config in the account. Review the compliance results for the EC2 instances and the container images."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability management service. It automatically discovers EC2 instances, container images, and Lambda functions, and it continually scans them for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure.",
      "GuardDuty detects active threats from logs and runtime activity. It does not inventory software vulnerabilities.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data in Amazon S3. It does not scan instances or container images.",
      "AWS Config evaluates resource configuration. It does not scan installed software for known vulnerabilities."] },

{ id: "d4-36", d: 4,
  q: "A company is implementing a data classification scheme. Every Amazon S3 bucket must be labeled Public, Internal, or Confidential, and only the security team can read Confidential buckets. The company also must detect buckets that have no label.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Tag each bucket with a DataClassification key, and find missing tags with the AWS Config required-tags rule.",
      "Use bucket policies or tag-based conditions that let only the security team's roles read Confidential buckets.",
      "Record each bucket's classification in a spreadsheet, and review it with the security team every quarter.",
      "Encrypt Confidential buckets with SSE-S3 to allow only the security team to decrypt the objects.",
      "Enable S3 Versioning for Confidential buckets to stop other users from reading the objects."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Tags are a common way to label resources with their classification. The AWS Config required-tags managed rule reports buckets that are missing the DataClassification key, so missing labels are found continuously.",
      "Access controls based on the classification, such as a bucket policy or a condition on aws:ResourceTag/DataClassification, make sure that only the security team's roles can read Confidential data. Encryption with a customer managed KMS key adds another layer.",
      "A spreadsheet is not linked to the resources and goes out of date quickly.",
      "S3 manages SSE-S3 keys for every authorized user, so SSE-S3 does not limit who can read the objects.",
      "Versioning keeps object versions. It does not restrict who can read objects."] },

{ id: "d4-37", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security team must receive an email notification whenever the root user of the company's AWS account is used. An AWS CloudTrail trail already delivers events to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a metric filter on the log group that matches root user activity. Create an alarm on the metric that notifies an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Enable AWS Config with the root-account-mfa-enabled managed rule. Send each rule compliance change to an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Create an IAM Access Analyzer external access analyzer. Send each new analyzer finding to an Amazon SNS topic.",
      "Review the AWS Trusted Advisor security checks each week. Send the summary to the security team's email address."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A metric filter on CloudTrail logs can match events where userIdentity.type is Root. An alarm on the resulting metric then notifies an SNS topic with an email subscription. AWS security standards recommend this exact control for root user use.",
      "This rule checks whether the root user has MFA. It does not report when the root user is used.",
      "Access Analyzer finds resources that are shared outside the zone of trust. It does not report root user activity.",
      "A weekly manual review does not notify the team whenever the root user is used."] },

{ id: "d4-38", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance that was created without encryption. A new policy requires all databases to be encrypted at rest with a customer managed AWS KMS key.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Take a snapshot, copy it with encryption using the KMS key, and restore a new DB instance from the copy.",
      "Modify the DB instance. Enable encryption with the KMS key. Apply the change immediately.",
      "Create an encrypted read replica of the unencrypted DB instance. Promote that read replica to primary.",
      "Enable default Amazon EBS encryption in the Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption cannot be enabled for an existing unencrypted RDS DB instance. Copying a snapshot with encryption and restoring it creates an encrypted instance. The application then moves to the new endpoint, so the change needs a planned cutover. An RDS blue/green deployment is a newer way to encrypt an unencrypted DB instance.",
      "RDS does not support enabling encryption for an existing unencrypted DB instance.",
      "RDS cannot create an encrypted read replica from an unencrypted DB instance.",
      "EBS encryption by default applies to EBS volumes that you create. It does not change RDS storage."] },

{ id: "d4-39", d: 4,
  q: "A company needs every new Amazon EBS volume in an AWS Region to be encrypted automatically. The company also has an unencrypted data volume, attached to a running instance, that must be encrypted.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Enable EBS encryption by default for the account in the Region.",
      "Snapshot the volume, create an encrypted volume from it, and swap the volumes.",
      "Run the modify-volume command with the encrypted option on the existing volume.",
      "Attach an IAM policy that denies the creation of unencrypted EBS volumes only.",
      "Enable Amazon S3 default encryption for the bucket that stores the EBS snapshots."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["EBS encryption by default is a Regional account setting. After it is enabled, all new volumes and snapshot copies in the Region are encrypted with the default KMS key or a key that you choose.",
      "An existing volume cannot be encrypted in place. Creating an encrypted volume from a snapshot, and then attaching it in place of the old volume, encrypts the data. The switch needs a short planned stop for the data volume.",
      "Elastic Volumes can change size, type, and performance, but it cannot encrypt an existing volume.",
      "A deny policy blocks unencrypted volumes, but it does not encrypt anything automatically, and it does nothing for the existing volume.",
      "EBS snapshots are not stored in a bucket that you manage, so S3 bucket settings do not apply."] },

{ id: "d4-40", d: 4,
  q: "A company has a data pipeline that writes millions of small objects every hour to an Amazon S3 bucket that uses SSE-KMS with a customer managed key. The pipeline receives ThrottlingException errors from AWS KMS, and KMS request costs are high.\n\nWhich solution will reduce the KMS requests with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Configure S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket's SSE-KMS encryption setting.",
      "Switch the bucket to dual-layer encryption with DSSE-KMS for every object.",
      "Create a second KMS key, and split the objects across the two keys at random.",
      "Request a higher limit for the S3 PUT request rate on the bucket's prefixes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With S3 Bucket Keys, S3 uses a short-lived bucket-level key from AWS KMS to create data keys for objects, instead of calling KMS for every object. This can reduce KMS requests and costs by up to 99%.",
      "Dual-layer encryption adds a second layer of encryption and increases KMS use instead of reducing it.",
      "Splitting traffic across keys needs application changes and does not reduce the total number of KMS requests, because request quotas are shared across keys in the account and Region.",
      "The errors come from AWS KMS, not from S3 request rates."] },

{ id: "d4-41", d: 4,
  q: "A company's compliance standard requires the key material of a customer managed AWS KMS key to be rotated every 180 days. The key was created with key material that AWS KMS generated.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Enable automatic key rotation for the key with a rotation period of 180 days.",
      "Create a new key every 180 days, and re-encrypt all of the data with the new key manually.",
      "Enable automatic rotation for the AWS managed key that the service uses by default.",
      "Schedule the key for deletion every 180 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automatic rotation for customer managed keys supports a custom rotation period from 90 to 2,560 days. KMS creates new key material on that schedule and keeps the older material to decrypt existing data, so nothing needs to be re-encrypted. On-demand rotation is also available.",
      "Manual rotation with re-encryption is a lot of work and is not needed for keys with KMS-generated key material.",
      "AWS managed keys rotate every year on a schedule that you cannot change.",
      "Deleting a key makes all data encrypted under it unrecoverable."] },

{ id: "d4-42", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an application that encrypts data in us-east-1 with an AWS KMS key and replicates the encrypted data to eu-west-1 for disaster recovery. The application in eu-west-1 must decrypt the data locally without calling the KMS key in us-east-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use a KMS multi-Region primary key in us-east-1 and a replica key in eu-west-1.",
      "Create a new single-Region key in eu-west-1. Use it to decrypt the replicated data.",
      "Export the key material from us-east-1, and import it into the AWS managed key in eu-west-1.",
      "Share the us-east-1 key with eu-west-1 by using AWS RAM."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multi-Region keys are related keys in different Regions that share the same key ID and key material. Data encrypted with the primary key in us-east-1 can be decrypted with the replica key in eu-west-1 without any cross-Region KMS calls.",
      "A different key cannot decrypt data that was encrypted under the original key.",
      "AWS KMS does not let you export key material that it generated, and AWS managed keys cannot import key material.",
      "KMS keys are not shared across Regions through AWS RAM."] },

{ id: "d4-43", d: 4,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer schedules a customer managed AWS KMS key for deletion with a 30-day waiting period because the company believes that the key is unused. The company must be alerted if anything tries to use the key during the waiting period.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a metric filter and alarm on AWS CloudTrail logs for requests that fail because the key is pending deletion.",
      "Enable automatic key rotation to write a new entry in AWS CloudTrail each time that the key is used.",
      "Disable the key instead, and wait for the users of the key to open a support ticket with the team.",
      "Shorten the waiting period to 7 days to find any problems with the deletion sooner."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["During the waiting period, the key cannot be used, and requests that try to use it fail and are recorded in CloudTrail. A metric filter on those events in CloudWatch Logs, with an alarm, alerts the team so that it can cancel the deletion if the key is still needed.",
      "Rotation creates new key material. It does not alert anyone about attempted use.",
      "Disabling a key first is a safer test, but waiting for tickets is not an alert and may come too late.",
      "A shorter waiting period gives less time to find problems before the key, and any data encrypted under it, is lost for good."] },

{ id: "d4-44", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an Amazon S3 bucket with default encryption that uses SSE-KMS with a specific customer managed key. Some applications upload objects with an explicit header that requests SSE-S3, which overrides the default. The company's security team requires every object to be encrypted with that KMS key.\n\nWhich solution will enforce this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies PutObject requests whose encryption headers do not specify the required KMS key.",
      "Enable S3 Bucket Keys to make all objects use the bucket-level key no matter which headers are sent.",
      "Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode to prevent objects from being uploaded with SSE-S3.",
      "Rely on the bucket's default encryption setting, because it always overrides any headers that applications send."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A bucket policy can deny PutObject requests unless the encryption headers name SSE-KMS and the required key, by using condition keys such as s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id. Uploads that ask for SSE-S3 or for a different key are rejected.",
      "S3 Bucket Keys reduce KMS request costs. They do not override an explicit request for SSE-S3.",
      "Object Lock protects object versions from deletion. It does not control encryption settings.",
      "Default encryption applies only when a request does not specify encryption. An explicit header overrides it."] },

{ id: "d4-45", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs a public website behind an Application Load Balancer that has only an HTTP listener on port 80. The company must serve all traffic over HTTPS with a free certificate that renews automatically. Users who type http:// must be sent to HTTPS.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Request a public ACM certificate with DNS validation. Add an HTTPS listener on port 443 that uses it.",
      "Change the port 80 listener's default action to redirect requests to HTTPS on port 443.",
      "Install a self-signed certificate on each Amazon EC2 instance. Keep the ALB listener on port 80.",
      "Import a certificate bought from a third-party CA into ACM, and renew it manually every year.",
      "Add a rule to the ALB security group that blocks inbound port 80 traffic from the internet."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Public ACM certificates are free, and ACM renews DNS-validated certificates automatically as long as the validation CNAME record stays in place. An HTTPS listener on the ALB terminates TLS with the certificate.",
      "A listener rule with a redirect action returns an HTTP 301 response to the HTTPS URL, so users who type http:// are moved to HTTPS.",
      "Browsers do not trust self-signed certificates, and the ALB would still serve plain HTTP to users.",
      "Imported certificates cost money from the CA and are not renewed automatically by ACM.",
      "Security groups cannot block traffic from the internet with a deny rule, and blocking port 80 would break users who type http:// instead of redirecting them."] },

{ id: "d4-46", d: 4,
  q: "AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) sends a company a notice that a DNS-validated public certificate used by an Amazon CloudFront distribution is not eligible for automatic renewal. Someone recently cleaned up old records in the domain's Amazon Route 53 hosted zone.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Re-create the ACM validation CNAME record in the hosted zone.",
      "Request a new certificate with email validation. Attach it to the distribution each year.",
      "Export the certificate from ACM, and import it again.",
      "Move the certificate to the Region of the CloudFront origin."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM renews DNS-validated certificates automatically only while the CNAME validation record is present and resolvable. Restoring the record lets ACM validate the domain again and renew the certificate before it expires.",
      "Email validation requires someone to approve each renewal, which adds manual work and risk.",
      "Re-importing does not extend a certificate's expiration date, and ACM does not renew imported certificates.",
      "Certificates for CloudFront must be in us-east-1, regardless of where the origin is."] },

{ id: "d4-47", d: 4,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer requested an AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) certificate for www.example.com in eu-west-1 and needs to use the certificate with an Amazon CloudFront distribution. The certificate does not appear in the certificate list in the CloudFront console.\n\nWhat should the CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Request or import the certificate in the us-east-1 Region, and select it in CloudFront.",
      "Wait 24 hours for the certificate to replicate from eu-west-1 to all CloudFront locations.",
      "Attach the certificate to an ALB in eu-west-1. Set that ALB as the CloudFront origin.",
      "Share the certificate with the CloudFront service principal by using AWS Resource Access Manager."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFront can use only ACM certificates that are in the US East (N. Virginia) Region, us-east-1. The certificate must be requested or imported there.",
      "ACM certificates do not replicate between Regions.",
      "A certificate on the origin load balancer secures traffic from CloudFront to the origin. It does not serve the viewer-facing domain on CloudFront.",
      "ACM certificates are not shared with CloudFront through AWS RAM."] },

{ id: "d4-48", d: 4,
  q: "An auditor finds that clients can download objects from a company's Amazon S3 bucket over plain HTTP. The company requires all requests to the bucket to use TLS.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy that denies all S3 actions when aws:SecureTransport is false.",
      "Enable default encryption with SSE-KMS to encrypt all requests in transit.",
      "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration to route requests over encrypted connections.",
      "Enable S3 Block Public Access to reject HTTP requests from the internet."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:SecureTransport is false for requests made over HTTP. A Deny statement with that condition rejects every unencrypted request, so clients must use HTTPS.",
      "Server-side encryption protects data at rest. It does not stop requests from using plain HTTP.",
      "Transfer Acceleration speeds up transfers. It does not block HTTP requests.",
      "Block Public Access controls public permissions. It does not decide whether authorized requests use TLS."] },

{ id: "d4-49", d: 4,
  q: "A company requires every connection to its Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance to use TLS. Some older clients still connect without TLS.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer enforce this requirement?",
  o: ["Set the rds.force_ssl parameter to 1 in the DB parameter group. Update the clients.",
      "Configure encryption at rest for the DB instance by restoring it from an encrypted snapshot copy.",
      "Add a security group rule that allows only port 443 to the DB instance from the application.",
      "Configure IAM database authentication."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The rds.force_ssl parameter makes the database reject connections that do not use SSL/TLS. Clients must be updated to connect with TLS and to trust the RDS certificate authority before the change, or they will fail to connect.",
      "Encryption at rest protects stored data. It does not force connections to use TLS.",
      "PostgreSQL listens on its own port, usually 5432. A port 443 rule does not control whether database connections use TLS.",
      "IAM authentication changes how users authenticate. It does not force other clients to use TLS."] },

{ id: "d4-50", d: 4,
  q: "A company imports certificates from a third-party certificate authority into AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for several load balancers. ACM does not renew imported certificates, and one of the certificates expired last month and caused an outage.\n\nWhich solution will warn the company at least 30 days before an imported certificate expires?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm on the ACM DaysToExpiry metric, or an Amazon EventBridge rule for expiration events.",
      "Enable automatic renewal for each imported certificate in the ACM console settings.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events for ACM to log each expiration in the trail.",
      "Create an Amazon Route 53 health check for each of the domains to fail on the day that the certificate expires."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM publishes the DaysToExpiry metric for certificates and sends approaching expiration events to EventBridge, starting 45 days before expiration by default. An alarm or rule can notify the team in time to renew the certificate and import it again. The acm-certificate-expiration-check Config rule is another option.",
      "ACM cannot renew imported certificates. They must be renewed with the issuing CA and imported again.",
      "Expiration is not an API call, so CloudTrail does not record it.",
      "A health check that fails on the expiration day gives no warning in advance."] },

{ id: "d4-51", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security standard requires an Application Load Balancer to accept only TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 with strong ciphers. A scan shows that the HTTPS listener still accepts TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Change the HTTPS listener's security policy to one that allows only TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.",
      "Import a new certificate into ACM that was issued with the TLS 1.3 protocol enabled for it.",
      "Add a security group rule that blocks clients that try to connect with TLS 1.0 or 1.1.",
      "Configure AWS WAF with a rule that blocks TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 requests at the listener."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The listener's security policy, such as ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06, sets which TLS versions and ciphers the load balancer negotiates. Choosing a policy that allows only TLS 1.2 and 1.3 stops the older protocols.",
      "Certificates are not tied to a TLS version. The protocol versions come from the listener's security policy.",
      "Security groups filter by IP address, port, and protocol. They cannot see TLS versions.",
      "AWS WAF inspects HTTP requests after TLS is negotiated, so it cannot control which TLS versions the listener accepts."] },

{ id: "d4-52", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an application that keeps the master password for an Amazon RDS for MySQL database in a configuration file. The password must be stored securely and rotated every 30 days without custom code.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST secure manner?",
  o: ["Let RDS manage the master user password in AWS Secrets Manager. Set the rotation schedule to 30 days.",
      "Store the password as a SecureString in Parameter Store. Enable its automatic rotation feature.",
      "Store the password in an encrypted Amazon S3 object. Replace it every 30 days by using a scheduled AWS Lambda script.",
      "Store the password in an AWS Lambda environment variable. Update the variable manually each month."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["When RDS manages the master user password, it stores the password in Secrets Manager and rotates it with managed rotation, with no Lambda function to write. The application reads the current value from Secrets Manager. The default rotation period is 7 days, and it can be changed.",
      "Parameter Store does not rotate SecureString parameters automatically.",
      "This is custom code, and the database password would still have to be changed and kept in sync correctly.",
      "Manual updates are error prone, and environment variables are not a secure secret store."] },

{ id: "d4-53", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an AWS Secrets Manager rotation function that runs in private subnets so that the function can reach an Amazon RDS database. Rotation fails, and the function's logs show timeouts when the function calls the Secrets Manager API. The subnets have no NAT gateway.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Create an interface VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager that allows HTTPS from the function's security group.",
      "Move the rotation function out of the VPC to reach both Secrets Manager and the database.",
      "Increase the rotation function's timeout from 30 seconds to 15 minutes to give the API call time to finish.",
      "Attach the SecretsManagerReadWrite policy to the database's IAM role to complete the rotation."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A function in private subnets without a NAT gateway has no path to the public Secrets Manager endpoint. An interface VPC endpoint gives it a private path, and the endpoint's security group must allow inbound HTTPS from the function.",
      "Outside the VPC, the function could reach Secrets Manager but not the private database.",
      "The calls time out because there is no network path, so a longer timeout only delays the failure.",
      "This is a network problem, not a permissions problem, and the database does not call Secrets Manager."] },

{ id: "d4-54", d: 4,
  q: "A company has a secret in AWS Secrets Manager in account A that stores API credentials. An application role in account B must read the secret. The secret is encrypted with the AWS managed key aws/secretsmanager.\n\nWhich solution will allow the role in account B to read the secret?",
  o: ["Encrypt the secret with a customer managed key. Allow account B in the key policy and the secret policy.",
      "Add a resource policy to the secret that allows account B to read it. Keep using the aws/secretsmanager key.",
      "Share the secret with account B by using AWS RAM. Keep using the aws/secretsmanager key for it.",
      "Replicate the secret to account B by using Secrets Manager replication to a Region in that account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Cross-account access to a secret needs a resource policy on the secret that allows the role, a customer managed KMS key whose key policy allows account B to decrypt, and an IAM policy in account B that allows GetSecretValue and kms:Decrypt. The policy of the AWS managed key cannot be changed to allow another account.",
      "The resource policy alone is not enough, because account B cannot use the AWS managed key.",
      "Secrets Manager uses resource policies, not AWS RAM, for cross-account access, and account B still could not use the AWS managed key.",
      "Replication copies a secret to other Regions in the same account, not to another account."] },

{ id: "d4-55", d: 4,
  q: "A company has a multi-Region application that reads database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager in us-east-1. During a disaster recovery test in us-west-2, the application cannot start because it cannot reach the secret.\n\nWhich solution will make the secret available in us-west-2 and keep it in sync with rotations?",
  o: ["Replicate the secret to us-west-2 with Secrets Manager multi-Region secret replication.",
      "Create a new secret in us-west-2 manually. Copy the value each time the secret rotates.",
      "Store a copy of the secret value in Parameter Store in us-west-2 as a String parameter.",
      "Grant the us-west-2 application access to the us-east-1 secret's AWS KMS key in that Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Secrets Manager can replicate a secret to other Regions. Replicas stay in sync when the primary secret's value changes, including through rotation, and the application can read the replica locally during a Regional outage.",
      "Manual copies drift out of sync after each rotation.",
      "A String parameter stores the credentials in plain text, and it does not stay in sync with rotations.",
      "The secret itself would still exist only in us-east-1, which might be unavailable during a disaster."] },

{ id: "d4-56", d: 4,
  q: "A company has an application that needs a third-party license key that never rotates, along with 30 other configuration values. The license key must be encrypted at rest at the lowest cost.\n\nWhere should a CloudOps engineer store the license key?",
  o: ["In Parameter Store as a SecureString standard parameter",
      "In AWS Secrets Manager with a 30-day automatic rotation schedule",
      "In a plaintext String parameter in Parameter Store",
      "In the application's source code repository"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SecureString parameters are encrypted with AWS KMS, and standard parameters have no storage charge. They suit secrets that do not need automatic rotation, such as a fixed license key.",
      "Secrets Manager charges for each secret every month and is built for rotation, which this key does not need.",
      "A String parameter is not encrypted, so the license key would be stored in plain text.",
      "Secrets in source code can leak through the repository and its history."] },

{ id: "d4-57", d: 4,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports a CryptoCurrency:EC2/BitcoinTool.B!DNS finding for a company's Amazon EC2 instance. For future findings of this type, the company's security team needs the instance isolated automatically and its disks preserved for forensics.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use an Amazon EventBridge rule on the finding type to start automation that isolates the instance and snapshots its volumes.",
      "Enable GuardDuty Malware Protection for EC2 to terminate the instance as soon as malware is found.",
      "Create a suppression rule for the finding type to stop the instance from sending cryptocurrency DNS queries.",
      "Add a GuardDuty trusted IP list that contains the instance's IP address to block its traffic."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty sends findings to EventBridge. A rule that matches the finding type can start a Systems Manager Automation runbook or a Lambda function that replaces the instance's security groups with an isolation security group and creates EBS snapshots for forensics.",
      "Malware Protection scans EBS volumes and reports findings. It does not terminate instances, and terminating would destroy evidence.",
      "Suppression rules hide findings from view. They do not stop the activity, and they would hide a real threat.",
      "Trusted IP lists tell GuardDuty not to generate findings for those addresses. They do not block traffic."] },

{ id: "d4-58", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs an approved vulnerability scanner from a fixed Amazon EC2 instance. Every day, Amazon GuardDuty generates many Recon:EC2/Portscan findings for this scanner, which hides real findings.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Create a GuardDuty suppression rule that matches the finding type and the scanner instance.",
      "Disable GuardDuty in the account during the hours of each day when the scanner is running.",
      "Archive each Portscan finding manually from the GuardDuty console after the security team reviews it.",
      "Add the scanner's IP address to a GuardDuty threat list."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Suppression rules automatically archive new findings that match criteria such as the finding type and the instance ID. The expected scanner findings no longer appear in the active list, while other findings are unaffected.",
      "Turning off GuardDuty leaves the account unmonitored for real threats during those hours.",
      "Archiving findings by hand every day is repetitive work that suppression rules automate.",
      "Threat lists contain known malicious IP addresses and would generate more findings, not fewer."] },

{ id: "d4-59", d: 4,
  q: "A company must detect threats in its AWS account, such as Amazon EC2 instances that communicate with known malicious IP addresses and unusual API activity. The solution must analyze existing AWS logs and must not require agents on the instances.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Enable Amazon GuardDuty in the account. Send the findings to the security team through Amazon EventBridge.",
      "Enable Amazon Inspector in the account. Send the findings to the security team through Amazon EventBridge.",
      "Enable Amazon Macie in the account. Send the findings to the security team through Amazon EventBridge.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events. Run a weekly Amazon Athena query to find unusual activity in the events."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty is a threat detection service that analyzes data sources such as CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs. It uses threat intelligence and machine learning, and its foundational detection needs no agents.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities and network exposure. It does not detect active threats such as communication with malicious IP addresses.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data in Amazon S3. It does not monitor instance traffic or API activity.",
      "This needs custom queries and a weekly review. It would not use threat intelligence to identify malicious IP addresses."] },

{ id: "d4-60", d: 4,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Inspector agent-based scanning for the Amazon EC2 instances in an account. Several instances show no vulnerability findings and are listed as unmanaged. SSM Agent is installed on these instances, but the instances have no instance profile.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Attach an instance profile with the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy to the instances.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring to send vulnerability data to Amazon Inspector.",
      "Enable AWS Config recording for the instances to allow Amazon Inspector to read their package list.",
      "Enable VPC Flow Logs for the instances to allow Amazon Inspector to find old software packages."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["For agent-based scanning, an instance must be managed by Systems Manager: SSM Agent must be running, and the instance needs permission. AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore provides that permission. With hybrid or agentless scanning enabled, Inspector can also scan unmanaged instances by using EBS snapshots.",
      "Runtime Monitoring detects threats in running workloads for GuardDuty. It does not supply package inventories to Inspector.",
      "Inspector does not use Config data to find software vulnerabilities.",
      "Flow logs record network traffic metadata. They do not show installed software versions."] },

{ id: "d4-61", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security team needs all accounts in the organization evaluated against the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard. The same standards and controls must be enabled in every account and AWS Region and must be managed from one place.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use AWS Security Hub CSPM central configuration from the delegated administrator with a configuration policy.",
      "Configure AWS Security Hub CSPM in each account and Region manually. Enable the standard in each one.",
      "Deploy a separate AWS Config rule for each control in the standard by using a StackSet for every account.",
      "Configure Amazon Inspector in every account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Central configuration lets the delegated administrator create configuration policies that enable Security Hub CSPM, standards, and controls for chosen accounts, OUs, and Regions. New accounts get the same settings automatically.",
      "Configuring every account and Region by hand is slow and drifts over time.",
      "Rebuilding the standard as separate Config rules is a large effort that Security Hub CSPM already provides.",
      "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities. It does not evaluate the best practices controls."] },

{ id: "d4-62", d: 4,
  q: "AWS Security Hub CSPM generates findings for a control whose risk a company has accepted for its development accounts only. These findings must be marked as suppressed automatically in development accounts, while the same findings stay active in production accounts.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an automation rule that matches the control in development accounts and sets the status to SUPPRESSED.",
      "Disable the control in every account and Region in the organization, including the production accounts.",
      "Archive each finding in the development accounts manually after it appears in the Security Hub console.",
      "Disable Security Hub CSPM in the development accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automation rules update findings as Security Hub receives them, based on criteria such as the control ID and the account ID. Setting the workflow status to SUPPRESSED for development accounts hides the accepted findings there, while production findings stay active.",
      "Disabling the control everywhere removes the check from production too.",
      "Manual handling is repetitive and easy to forget.",
      "Turning off Security Hub CSPM removes all of the other checks in those accounts."] },

{ id: "d4-63", d: 4,
  q: "A company's security team receives separate findings from Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, Amazon Macie, and AWS Security Hub CSPM. The security team needs these signals correlated to show which resources face the highest risk.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Enable AWS Security Hub, which correlates the signals into exposures and prioritizes risks.",
      "Export all findings to Amazon S3, and write Amazon Athena queries that join them by resource ID.",
      "Enable AWS Config aggregators.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch dashboard that shows the finding counts for each service side by side."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Security Hub correlates signals from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, and Security Hub CSPM and identifies exposures, such as a reachable resource with an exploitable vulnerability. It prioritizes risks so that the team can act on the most important issues first.",
      "Custom queries can join findings, but building and maintaining risk correlation logic is a large effort.",
      "Config aggregators combine configuration and compliance data. They do not correlate security findings from these services.",
      "Counts side by side do not show which specific resources combine several risks."] },

{ id: "d4-64", d: 4,
  q: "A company needs to test a new customer-facing web application for exploitable vulnerabilities before each major release. The company has no penetration testing staff and needs on-demand tests that take the application's design and code into account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use AWS Security Agent to run on-demand penetration tests against the application.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty for the account to test the application for attacks.",
      "Enable Amazon Inspector to perform penetration tests on the web application.",
      "Enable AWS Shield Advanced to simulate attacks against the application."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Security Agent performs on-demand penetration testing that uses context from the application's design documents, source code, and infrastructure as code. It reports exploitable vulnerabilities with guidance for fixing them, and it also offers design and code security reviews.",
      "GuardDuty detects threats from activity in the account. It does not attack your application to find vulnerabilities.",
      "Inspector scans for known software vulnerabilities and network reachability. It does not perform penetration tests of application logic.",
      "Shield Advanced protects against DDoS attacks. It does not test applications for vulnerabilities."] },

{ id: "d4-65", d: 4,
  q: "A company's administrators use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to access Amazon EC2 instances. Auditors require a record of every command typed in each session, and the session data must be encrypted with a key that the company controls.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Configure Session Manager preferences to send session logs to Amazon S3 or Amazon CloudWatch Logs.",
      "Configure AWS KMS encryption of session data with a customer managed key in the preferences.",
      "Open port 22 on the instances to capture session commands by using VPC Flow Logs.",
      "Enable AWS CloudTrail data events to record every command that is typed in a session.",
      "Install the Amazon CloudWatch agent to capture the keyboard input of each session."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Session Manager can upload or stream session output, including the commands that are run, to an S3 bucket or a CloudWatch Logs log group for auditing.",
      "Session Manager preferences can require that session data is encrypted with a customer managed KMS key, in addition to the TLS encryption that protects data in transit.",
      "Session Manager does not use SSH, and flow logs record only traffic metadata, not commands.",
      "CloudTrail records the StartSession API call and who started it, but not the commands typed inside the session.",
      "The CloudWatch agent collects metrics and log files. It is not the way to record session activity."] },
]);
